.TH CHARBUF_PRED_SKIP 3 "2023-04-01" "1.0" "lib/charbuf/charbuf_pred_skip"
.SH NAME
charbuf_pred_skip \- Skip characters in a character buffer based on a predicate
.SH SYNOPSIS
#include <lib/charbuf.h>
#include <lib/stralloc.h>

ssize_t charbuf_pred_skip(charbuf *b, int (*pred)(int, size_t, void*), void *ptr);
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBcharbuf_pred_skip\fR function skips characters in the character buffer \fBb\fR based on a predicate function \fBpred\fR. The predicate function is called for each character in the buffer, and if it returns non-zero, the character is skipped. The function continues to skip characters until the predicate returns zero or there are no more characters to skip.

This function is useful for skipping a sequence of characters in a buffer that satisfy a certain condition.
.SH ARGUMENTS
\fBb\fR
The character buffer from which to skip characters.
\fBpred\fR
A predicate function that determines whether a character should be skipped.
\fBptr\fR
A pointer to data that will be passed to the predicate function.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The number of characters skipped.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBcharbuf_pred_skip\fR function:
.nf
#include <lib/charbuf.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int is_alpha(int c, size_t len, void *ptr) {
    (void)ptr; // Unused
    return (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') || (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z');
}

int main() {
    charbuf b;
    charbuf_init(&b);
    charbuf_append(&b, "Hello, world!");

    size_t count = charbuf_pred_skip(&b, is_alpha, NULL);

    printf("Number of alphabetic characters skipped: %zu\n", count);

    charbuf_free(&b);

    return 0;
}
.ne
.fi
